## Feature #4 Increment 1 (spec §4.4) — async backpressured `Queue`, basic
## bounded send/recv over the per-thread reactor.
##
## `Queue.bounded(cap, Overflow.Suspend)` returns a `QueueEnds` handle; pull
## the typed `sender`/`receiver` ends off it. Both share one refcounted C
## control block (a ring buffer of capacity `cap`). `send`/`recv` are
## HAND-WRITTEN futures (async methods on classes aren't lowered) returning
## `Result[Int, QueueError]`, shaped like `AsyncReadWithTimeoutFuture`
## (`queue_ptr`/`done` + `include Future` + `def var poll` + `def drop`). On
## would-block they record the parked task's WAKER on the queue's wake slot and
## return `Poll.Pending`; the peer's enqueue/dequeue wakes the parked TASK via
## the reactor — the TASK parks, the OS thread NEVER blocks (unlike the sync
## MPSC `Receiver.recv`, which `pthread_cond_wait`s the whole thread).
##
## Shape: three single-send producer tasks (each sends one value then
## completes) are spawned; the consumer is the `block_on` ROOT that recvs three
## values and sums them (10+20+30 = 60). The consumer's first recv parks on the
## empty queue; each producer's send wakes it. Proves: split ends, the shared
## control block, ring enqueue/dequeue, the Result[Int, QueueError] channel, and
## reactor park/wake (NOT thread block).
##
## Why single-send producer tasks (not one 3-send task): a SPAWNED multi-await
## state machine does not self-reschedule when an intermediate `.await` resolves
## Ready and the machine advances+yields Pending — the linear-N dispatch returns
## `Poll.Pending` without re-waking its own task, so the pump (which gates on
## the task's ready bit) never re-polls it. A `block_on` ROOT is re-polled every
## loop iteration regardless, so a MULTI-await future works fine AS THE ROOT
## (the consumer here) — only SPAWNED multi-await tasks strand. This is a
## pre-existing async-lowering limitation (spawned-multi-await self-wake),
## documented as a v1 follow-up; the queue itself parks/wakes correctly.
##
## v1: SINGLE-REACTOR (all tasks on one worker/reactor — cross-core wake is
## deferred to v2, spec §4.4 risk 3 / §6). Int element channel (the established
## Task/Race/Scope convention). `.await` on the no-arg `recv` is spelled
## `recv()` (empty-paren method call → MethodCall awaitee Shape-3).

async def producer_one(tx: AsyncSender, value: Int) -> Int
  let _a = tx.send(value).await
  0
end

async def consumer(rx: AsyncReceiver) -> Int
  let r1 = rx.recv().await
  let r2 = rx.recv().await
  let r3 = rx.recv().await
  let v1 = unwrap_or_neg(r1)
  let v2 = unwrap_or_neg(r2)
  let v3 = unwrap_or_neg(r3)
  v1 + v2 + v3
end

## A recv Result → its Int value, or -1 on QueueClosed. A free fn so the
## consumer's await segments stay simple `let`s (the accepted linear-N shape).
def unwrap_or_neg(r: Result[Int, QueueError]) -> Int
  match r
    Ok(v)  -> v
    Err(_) -> -1
  end
end

def main
  puts "queue start"
  var ends = Queue.bounded(4, Overflow.Suspend)
  let rx = ends.receiver
  let tx1 = ends.sender
  let tx2 = tx1.share
  let tx3 = tx1.share
  let _h1 = Task.spawn_raw(producer_one(tx1, 10))
  let _h2 = Task.spawn_raw(producer_one(tx2, 20))
  let _h3 = Task.spawn_raw(producer_one(tx3, 30))
  let sum = block_on(consumer(rx))
  puts "sum=#{sum}"
  puts "queue done"
end
